  "review": [
    {
      "section": "time.clock",
      "confidence": "low",
      "note": "Shona has no past/to system in common Shona-only speech (people code-switch to English). Used the idiomatic answer to 'Dzava nguvai?': hours carry the copula so 1 o'clock gets the class-9 singular ('yava imwe') and the rest class 10 ('dzava mbiri' ...). Minutes additive with class-6 numerals: '{h} nemaminitsi gumi', 'makumi maviri', 'makumi mana nemashanu' ...; '{h} nehafu' for half. Compromise: every phrase reads as 'it is now ...', which is natural as an answer to 'what time is it' but awkward inside a sentence ('at ...'). Also changed midnight 'usiku hwepakati' (unidiomatic) to 'pakati peusiku'. Doubts: whether 'nekota' would be preferred for :15/:45; the copula framing itself."
    }
  ]
